## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, smart materials, novel synthesis, advanced materials, composite materials, green chemistry
## ABSTRACT
Advances in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) resulted in significant contributions to diverse applications such as carbon capture, gas storage, heat transformation and separation along with emerging applications toward catalysis, medical imaging, drug delivery, and sensing. The unique in situ and ex situ structural features of MOFs can be tailored by conceptual selection of the organic (e.g., ligand) and inorganic (e.g., metal) components. Here, we provide a comprehensive review on the synthesis and characterization of MOFs, particularly with respect to controlling their size and morphology. A better understanding of the specific size and morphological parameters of MOFs will help initiate a new era for their real-world applications. Most importantly, this assessment will help develop novel synthesis methods for MOFs and their hybrid/porous materials counterparts with considerably improved properties in targeted applications.
## 1 Introduction
Remarkable advances have been achieved in crystal engineering and coordination chemistry since the beginning of the 1990s. Lehn [1] first introduced the concept of "supramolecular chemistry" during the Nobel Prize award ceremony in 1987. A decade later, Yaghi et al. [2] reported the structure of MOF-5, a metal-organic framework (MOF), for the first time, initiating a new era for the development of supramolecular chemistry with nanoporosity and high surface area. This development provided important insights into the possible assembly of organic and inorganic building blocks for porous structures in both the design and construction stages. In this context, chemists with background in the synthesis of zeolite and related porous materials began
organic framework (MOF), for the first time, initiating a new era for the development of supramolecular chemistry with nanoporosity and high surface area. This development provided important insights into the possible assembly of organic and inorganic building blocks for porous structures in both the design and construction stages. In this context, chemists with background in the synthesis of zeolite and related porous materials began
to consider the use of organic molecules as reactants for the synthesis of this framework structure. As part of such efforts, MOFs have been proposed as an important sub-class of hybrid porous materials, consisting of metal connecting points and organic bridging ligands with predictable structures [2–11].
Over the past few decades, MOFs have attracted great attention due to their intrinsically tunable structures, flexible architectures, high pore volumes, large surface areas, and multifunctional properties. In light of such properties, MOFs have often been preferred materials for diverse fields of applications including gas storage [12–17], separation [18–25], catalysis [26–31], and drug delivery [32–38].
The enhanced structural flexibility of MOFs provided an opportunity for alterations in their size and morphology. In this regard, enormous efforts have been made to modify the structural architecture of MOFs [38–41]. Moreover, the control of the main structural components such as metal ion and organic ligand molecules can provide a fundamental understanding of the crystallization mechanisms of this new class of porous materials [42, 43]. In order to optimize the properties of MOFs for specific applications, various post-synthetic approaches have been developed [44–46].
The methods used for the synthesis of MOFs were found to be an important controlling factor to achieve the uniform porous structures of these materials. Therefore, (1) different synthesis techniques, including conventional solvothermal synthesis [47–49], microwave-assisted synthesis [50–52], electrochemical synthesis [53–55], and ultrasound-promoted crystallization [56–58]; (2) effective control of synthetic parameters, such as reaction time [44, 59, 60], temperature [61–63], solvents [64–66], and molar ratio of reactants [67–69]; and (3) use of size modulators, such as capping agents [70–74] were considered as some of the best ways to optimize the morphology of MOFs. The availability of diverse structural modification options helped us broaden the possibilities for the synthesis of diverse MOFs. Such developments have been truly beneficial in the investigation of the diverse functional properties of MOFs and their composite materials [75–77].
The main goal of this review is to describe the fundamental aspects of MOF technology in terms of synthesis and controlling variables, with respect to size
and morphology. Essentially, the concepts of miniaturization and parallelization in combinations (inorganic and organic unit) have allowed us not only to offer deeper insights into MOFs, but also to facilitate the adoption of novel synthesis trends. Likewise, the kinetics of MOF crystallization should be properly assessed for a better understanding of the nucleation and growth process of MOFs. Such essential and complex relationships of synthesis components account for the novel isoreticular series and discovery of new MOFs.
The basic approaches for controlling the size and morphology of MOFs will be further described in order to expand the possible fields of their applications. Therefore this review focuses on the major challenges and strategies for such modulation of MOFs. Lastly, the future prospects of MOFs are further assessed, especially with respect to their synthesis technology.
## 2 Progress on investigations of MOF synthesis
The presence of a large void volume in the interior pores of MOFs is due to diverse combinations of vertices and linkers of MOFs which contribute to the various topologies and pore sizes of these materials [78]. The adsorption of \(CO_{2}\) in MOFs was regulated by both physisorption and chemisorption; namely, physisorption is temperature and pressure dependent, while chemisorption is mainly due to the acid-base neutralization reaction [79]. Contrastingly, \(H_{2}\) was mainly physisorbed on the surface of MOF pores. Hence, the \(H_{2}\) sorption capability of many MOFs was noticed to be reduced considerably as they tend to interact weakly with \(H_{2}\) molecules through van der Waals forces at low pressure and ambient temperature [80–82]. Other than chemisorption, the most effective way to improve the sorption capability is to maximize the effective occupancy of void volume and to optimize pore size and shape. Moreover it was demonstrated that the \(H_{2}\) uptake exhibited a positive correlation with these properties of MOFs [83–85]. As such, the \(H_{2}\) uptake capacities should be tightly affected by the surface area properties of MOFs [82]. Thus, these kinds of required structural parameters can potentially be tuned up by selecting the most suitable synthesis technique for MOFs.
The synthesis of MOFs was originally developed based on a thorough evaluation of traditional framework
materials, such as zeolite, with respect to morphology and elemental compositions. Through modification of such principles, many synthetic approaches have been developed, including hydrothermal, solvothermal, sonochemical, microwave-assisted and many others. As an alternative to traditional methods (e.g., hydrothermal and solvothermal methods), ultrasound- or microwave-assisted synthesis was suggested to effectively alter the crystallization behavior of MOFs [86–88]. However, these newer approaches have been found to lack reproducibility and throughput, therefore the sizes of particles are ultimately affected [89, 90]. In order to solve these issues, various methods have been introduced, such as direct mixing [91], mechanochemical synthesis [92], and free-/wet-drying approaches [93]. In this section, several case studies were assessed to allow direct comparison between conventional and modern synthesis routes for MOFs preparation. In addition, we provided information regarding the morphology,
surface area, and pore volume of the synthesized products in each section. To gain a better understanding of the synthesis methods crucial to the morphology of the final product, one may refer to Masoomi and Morsali's article [75].
## 2.1 Conventional synthesis
## 2.1.1 Hydrothermal synthesis
Nano-scale MOFs have been used in drug delivery, imaging, and sensing of biological molecules [94–96]. A controlled synthesis method has been extensively evaluated for the preparation of nano-scale MOFs with high dispersibility. Guo et al. [97] introduced a hydrothermal-based synthesis method using glycerol as a size-directing agent. Glycerol slowed down the rate of crystal growth and caused the miniaturization of chromium-benzenedicarboxylates (MIL-101) crystal size (Fig. 1(a)). With the addition of 30 to \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) glycerol,
the particle size of MIL-101 decreased dramatically from 500 to \(80\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1(a)). However, the effect on the particle size scissoring properties was no longer evident if glycerol was added after the synthesis of MIL-101 (Figs. 1(b) and 1(c)). It was thus concluded that glycerol acts as an inhibitor of fast nucleation during hydrothermal synthesis, as it provides more hydroxyl groups to the MIL-101 (Table 1). Furthermore, the addition of the hydroxyl groups helped increase the monodispersity of MIL-101, making bio-molecular sensing applications of these materials feasible [97]. The authors thus found that the size of nanocrystals \((80{-}500\ \mathrm{nm})\) was moderately larger than that of the \(50\,\mathrm{nm}\) crystals generated by Khan et al. [50]. In addition, the crystal size of the final product decreased when water was added to the reaction mixture, which was mainly attributed to an increased nucleation rate at lower dilution [50].
## 2.1.2 Solvothermal synthesis
The strategy used to increase the van der Waals interactions between the framework and guest molecules was based on the construction of highly porous MOFs with tunable, narrow pore size and irregular pore shape. In this regard, a new class of Cu-based MOFs possessing \(\alpha\mathrm{-PbO}_{2}\) (apo) topology with zigzag-shaped channels was designed through solvothermal synthesis [12]. Unlike other MOFs, the zigzag channels of [CuL]- DMF- \(2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}=5\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-}1,2,4\mathrm{-}\) triazol-4-yl)benzene-1,3- dicarboxylic acid, \(\mathrm{DMF}=\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}\) -dimethylformamide) with narrow pore size \((5.6\mathrm{~\AA~}\times4.6\mathrm{~\AA~})\) enabled a stronger contact with light gases such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(C O_{2},\) with maximum sorption capacities of \(1.46\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) and \(22\,\mathrm{wt.\%},\) respectively.
The main factors determining the morphology of the final product in the solvothermal process are as follows: a) the nature of salt hydration, b) the metal/ ligand (M/L) ratio, c) the reaction time and temperature, and d) the stirring parameters. The influence of those four parameters in the synthesis of MOF-5 has been extensively investigated [45]. These authors revealed that the formation of pure-phase MOF-5 proceeded mainly as a two-step process at temperatures above \(130\,^{\circ}C.\) Moreover, metastable intermediate solid phases were produced initially from the solution phase of metal and ligand (Fig. 2(a)) [45]. At temperatures below \(120\,^{\circ}C,\)
stirring had a noticeable influence on the formation of MOF-5 (e.g., bulk phases of MOF-5). As the M/L ratio exceeded 1.33, the formation of MOF-5 was prevented by the presence of excess terephthalic acid [45] (Fig. 2(b)). Nonetheless, the addition of water during the synthesis of MOF-5 produced MOF-69c, while the product could still form MOF-5 after the addition of terephthalic acid [45, 98] as follows
\[
4\mathrm{Zn}_3(\mathrm{OH})_2(\mathrm{BDC})_2+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{BDC}\leftrightarrow3\mathrm{Zn}_4\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_3+5\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}
\]
MOF-69c(desolv) \(^+\) terephthalic acid \(\leftrightarrow\mathrm{MOF}{-}5+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)
where BDC is 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid.
Some of the novel ligand-based MOFs have also been synthesized using a solvothermal approach. For example, \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})],\) where DABCO is 1,4- diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane and other transition metal-based MOFs such as \([\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]\mathrm{DMF},\) \([\mathrm{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]\mathrm{DMF},\) and \([\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}]\mathrm{DMF}\) have been synthesized using DABCO and formic acid as ligand molecules [20, 99] (Table 1). It should be noted that \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]\) exhibited a high Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of \(2{,}703\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) to facilitate gas sorption [99]. On the other hand, transition metal and formic acid-based MOFs formed hexagonal disks with rod-shaped morphologies [20]. These materials exhibited extremely high separation potential between ethylbenzene and p-xylene molecules via pressure swing adsorption. The separation mechanism of these MOFs was found to reflect the difference in diffusion kinetic parameters of the analytes on the MOF surface [20].
Solvent molecules are important components in MOF synthesis that initially reside inside the MOFs to help maintain the neutrality of their frameworks. Hence, the effect of solvent molecules on the morphology of MOFs needs to be thoroughly investigated [52]. The properties of a series of framework isomers based on Zn and Pb have been evaluated based on conventional synthesis methods (Table 1) [48, 65]. The results indicated that the use of different solvent systems was involved in the formation of different architectures. The solvent system used for the preparation of \([\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]_{n},\) \([\mathrm{Pb}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{3}]_{n},\) and \([\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{L})]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) \(\mathrm{MeOH/H_{2}O}\) and MeOH, respectively, where MeOH is methanol [65]. Here, \([\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]_{n}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{L}){\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) possessed the architecture of multi-nodal 3,3,4,4-connected to an
uninodal 3-connected net, whereas \(\left[\mathrm{Pb}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{3}\right]_{n}\) possessed a binodal 3,4-connected topological net. In addition, the MeOH-based MOF system remained microporous along with the uncoordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms. In contrast, the solvent systems using \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{MeOH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) did not hold any micropores. Thus, the porous nature of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{L})\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) has been utilized favorably for the storage of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with an estimated storage capacity of \(1.27\;\mathrm{mmol\cdot g^{-1}}\).
## 2.2 Modern synthesis
## 2.2.1 Microwave synthesis
In general, solvothermal and hydrothermal processes are relatively slow and require high operating temperatures [98–100]. However, some solvothermal processes can also proceed using surface catalyzing agents such as triethyl amine (TEA) and hexadecyltrimethyl ammonium bromide (CTAB) for efficient MOF formation [101]. Nonetheless, the presence of an improper ratio of these agents may lead to the production of more different crystalline structures than expected [102]. In recent
years, microwave and ultrasonic irradiation have been introduced for the synthesis of MOF materials. Such methods have drawn a good deal of attention due to the homogeneous heating, fast kinetics, high phase purity, and high yield rate of products [103, 104].
Among the MOFs reported to date, MIL-101 is one of the most widely studied due to its mesoporous structure and extremely high pore volume. Many efforts have been made to modulate the size of MIL-101 [3, 50, 103]. For instance, Khan et al. [50] compared the synthesis methods of conventional electric heating and microwave irradiation and also investigated the influences of varying water concentration and \(\mathrm{pH}\) values on the reaction medium. Increases in both water content and \(\mathrm{pH}\) contributed to the decrease in size of MIL-101. Furthermore, \(50\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{MIL}{-}101\) crystals were obtained using microwave irradiation at a \(\mathrm{pH}\) of 4.2. In comparison, larger sized MIL-101 \((100{-}800\ \mathrm{nm})\) was also synthesized using a conventional electric heating method over an extended time (at least \(6\ \mathrm{h}\)) at a \(\mathrm{pH}\) ranging from 2.7 to 4.0.
A microwave-assisted non-solvothermal synthesis method was proposed to prepare HKUST-1 [105]. The textural properties and yields of the final products were sensitive to several variables, such as concentration of metal ions and ligand molecules, solvent, temperature, reaction time, and microwave power. The increase in the reactant concentration led to increased BET surface area and a decreased product yield. The surface area drastically decreased when \(\mathrm{EtOH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (where \(\mathrm{EtOH}\) is ethyl alcohol) or DMF were used as solvents. Notably, the textural property of HKUST-1 improved when the temperature was increased from 60 to \(70\ ^{\circ}C\), but deteriorated noticeably above \(80\ ^{\circ}C\). The surface area of these crystals was observed to increase as the reaction time increased (e.g., from 5 to \(10\ \mathrm{min}\)). Finally, pure-phase HKUST-1 with a surface area of up to \(1,860\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) was synthesized using the optimized synthesis method (630 W, \(70\ ^{\circ}C\), and \(10\ \mathrm{min}\)) using a solvent mixture of DMF, \(\mathrm{EtOH}\), and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with a 1:1:1 volume ratio.
The use of microwave-assisted synthesis was first reported for three known isoreticular MOFs, namely IRMOF-1, IRMOF-2, and IRMOF-3 (Fig. 3) [88]. High-quality MOF crystals were successfully produced in less than 1 min by microwave irradiation, while the conventional solvothermal method took between 1 to 7
days. IRMOF-1 particles with an average size of \(4 \pm 1\) \(\upmu\mathrm{m}\) were found to be microsized cubic crystals, while IRMOF-2 and 3 appeared to have cubic morphology but at a different size. In addition, smaller crystals could be produced with just the reduction of the reactant concentration in the initial solution. Notably, for the synthesis of IRMOF-1 crystals, only a slight change in the concentration of BDC caused obvious changes in size, while the M:L molar ratio remained 4:3 (Fig. 4).
\(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) is one of the most studied MOFs due to its wide range of applicability [90]. Development of a suitable synthesis method for this MOF can help further expand its applicability. To learn more about the effects of synthesis conditions, Schlesinger et al. [90] adopted six different synthesis methods: a) ambient pressure with ultrasonication, b) electrochemical,
c) mechanochemical, d) solvent-assisted mechanochemical, e) microwave-assisted, and f) microwave-assisted solvothermal synthesis. Among those, microwave-assisted solvothermal synthesis was found to be the most efficient method for the production of high pore volume \((0.79\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) crystals with a relatively shorter synthesis time \((30\;\mathrm{min})\) [90]. The second-best method was the solvent-assisted mechanochemical one, resulting in a similar pore volume compared to the above method \((0.74\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). In contrast, electrochemical synthesis gave much poorer results for the obtained products: low yield, low surface area, and low crystallinity [90]. Thus, microwave-assisted synthesis appears to be an effective technology for producing large amounts of MOFs with well-defined morphologies.
## 2.2.2 Electrochemical synthesis
Electrochemical synthesis of MOFs is an attractive area of research due to the fast reactivity of metal ions with ligand molecules, as well as the well-defined product morphology. Electrochemical synthesis is based on supplying the metal ion, via anodic dissolution, to a synthesis solution containing the organic linker in an electrolyte [106]. This way, electrochemical synthesis of CuTATB \((\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TATB}=4,4',4''-\mathrm{s-triazine}-2,4,6-\mathrm{trityl}-\mathrm{tribenzoic}\) acid) has been used to produce two different forms of products: powder and surface-grown MOFs [54]. A clear distinction was observed between the bulk powder and surface-grown MOFs. The powder sample consisted of agglomerated particles grown with sizes ranging from nano- to micrometers with a BET surface area of \(570\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Figs. 5(a) and 5(c)). On the other hand, CuTATB grown on copper mesh showed the formation of much smaller MOF particles with the two-step isotherm process for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption (Figs. 5(b) and 5(d)). The hysteresis of CuTATB grown on a copper surface was due to the presence of an interpenetrated cage structure in the molecular network. It should be noted that this electrochemical process is a salt-free synthesis process, therefore, the unnecessary formation of internal impurities can be avoided.
Since DMF and N,N-diethylformamide (DEF) are solvents with well-known toxicity for humans and the environment, the electrochemical synthesis of MOF-5 was also explored to replace such solvents with ionic liquids (IL) [53]. Here, IL was used as a templating
agent that can offer a suitable phase for the interaction between the cationic component of the reagent and the anionic part of the IL [53]. In the electrochemical synthesis of MOF-5, a unique flower-shaped morphology with a diameter of approximately \(10\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) was produced using of 1-butyl-3-methylimidazole (Bmim) as an IL (Fig. 6). This unique morphology of MOF-5 with high phase purity was attributed to two types of interactions: a) the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction between the imidazole groups of the IL and b) the ionic bond between \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) [107]. Interestingly, according to the cyclic voltammetric curve, electrochemically synthesized MOF-5 was surprisingly effective for the hydrogen evolution reaction due to the abundance of electron holes in the flower-shaped structure [107].
## 2.2.3 Ultrasonication
Sonochemical methods are widely used for the preparation
of many organic suspensions [108]. Nonetheless, the applicability of ultrasonication in the preparation of
MOFs is still in its infancy. Ultrasonication has been used for the synthesis of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) at ambient temperature and pressure [57]. The obtained product yield was \(62.6\%{-}85.1\%\) with an ultrasonication time of \(5{-}60\ \mathrm{min}\). In addition, the dimensions of the obtained nanocrystals were in the \(10{-}200\ \mathrm{nm}\) range, which are smaller than those of conventional solvothermal origin. Additionally, the BET surface areas resulting from 30 and \(60\ \mathrm{min}\) of ultrasonication were 1,075 and \(1,100\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively [57]. The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption capacities of these MOFs increased slightly from \(1.76\ \mathrm{wt.}\%\) to \(1.93\ \mathrm{wt.}\%\) with the increase in sonication time from 30 to \(60\ \mathrm{min}\) [57]. Therefore, ultrasonication was found to be another efficient method for the synthesis of nano-scale MOFs. However, care should be taken to obtain a well-defined morphology during the mixing of the solvent and the reflux time of the reaction. Khoshhal et al. [109] employed ultrasonication followed by reflux method to synthesize Cu-BTC MOFs, and studied the effect of reaction temperature and solvent content on the structure and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage performance of the MOFs. It was found that the crystallinity of the products decreased as the reaction temperature increased. The final product obtained using \(75\ \mathrm{mL}\) of DMF at \(80\,^{\circ}\!C\) possessed the highest BET surface area: \(1,386.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
High quality MOF-5 particles were obtained using a sonochemical methodology, which dramatically reduced the reaction time to minutes, compared to \(12\ \mathrm{h}\) for conventional solvothermal synthesis [110]. Typically, MOF-5 was synthesized by dissolving \(40\ \mathrm{mL}\) of 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) with a molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O{\cdot}H_{2}BDC{\cdot}NMP=1{\cdot}0.33{\cdot}(25{-}90)}\). It should be noted that the Langmuir surface areas of MOF-5 resulting from the above two synthesis methods were almost identical at 3,208 and \(3,200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The MOF-5 crystals synthesized by both methods also exhibited similar capacities for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption, which were notably larger than that of the zeolite 13X material.
## 2.2.4 Thin films and membrane growth
The key prerequisite for the preparation of thin films is the proper coating of the nanoparticle onto a suitable substrate. The particle size of the thin film determines the diffusion rate of the entire active material during sensing processes by minimizing the response time
[111, 112]. To prepare uniform and ultrasmall MOF particles (e.g., HKUST-1 and IRMOF-3), a strategy for additive-mediated synthesis was applied and found to be effective [113]. In this approach, the ligand was initially mixed with the polymer or a polymer-surfactant combination to react with the metal ion, which yielded MOFs in nanoparticle form. These nanoparticles were later used for the preparation of uniform HKUST-1 and IRMOF-3 thin films with thicknesses of 173 and \(147\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Fig. 7).
The deposition of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) seeds on the tuber surface using chitosan as an organic binder was carried out using a dip coating method for membrane preparation [39]. In this regard, hollow ceramic fibers (HCFs) with a pore size of \(200\ \mathrm{nm}\) have been used as a substrate. In addition, the HCFs were found to be sufficient for placing \(\sim300\ \mathrm{nm}\) MOF seeds on the substrate without any pore clogging. After the membrane was grown, chitosan was used as a pore-functionalizing material to help improve the binding force between the MOF seeds and the substrate due to its abundance of hydroxyl and amino groups [39]. The thickness of the membrane through this process led to the growth of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) on the HCFs (e.g., up to \(13\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) thickness). The prepared material, when used for the separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in binary mixtures, exhibited selectivities of 8.66, 13.6, and 6.19 for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), respectively. The high selectivity, robustness, durability, and diffusivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) achieved through this dip coating-assisted chitosan method make it an alternative and effective MOF for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) separation.
The step-by-step growth of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) on different substrates for application in quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) has been investigated [42]. For this purpose, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was coated onto different surfaces (e.g., \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and Au functionalized with COOH- and OH-terminated self-assembled monolayers (SAM)) over 40 cycles (Fig. 8(a)) [42]. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images revealed that \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) coating on \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) produced a dense structure with a regular smooth surface (surface roughness of about \(6.4\ \mathrm{nm}\)) (Fig. 8(b)). On the other hand, the measured surface roughness for \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) deposited on \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and COOH- and OH-terminated SAMs was in the \(16{-}17\ \mathrm{nm}\) range (Fig. 8(b)) [42]. Thus, the uniform \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) thin film deposited on \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was found to be suitable for QCM applications.
## 2.3 Post-synthetic modifications
2.3 Post-synthetic modificationsIn general, most MOFs synthesized using the procedures described above are made of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites with mono- or bi-functionalized ligands. Based on their synthetic routes, some MOFs are identified as ideal materials for sorption and separation applications. However, those materials encountered a series of structural problems such as their weakness to acidity, complicated activation processes (removal of pore-filling solvents without damage of the MOF), and limitation in applicability to various fields (e.g., the damage of frameworks due to moisture) [26]. To solve this problem, material chemists and engineers created a method of using multifunctional ligand units allowing their end products to possess a wide variety of desired characteristics. In this respect, post-synthetic modification (PSM) has become a popular option for tailoring the molecular building blocks of the framework. The general approach used for the PSM process is as follows: 1) addition of multiple ligands with different functional sites during the pre-synthesis process, 2) PSM of monofunctional ligands by tandem grafting different groups, and 3) PSM of the bi-functional ligand through separate reactions [26]. As a result, PSM broadens the horizon for more convenient applications of MOFs toward catalysis, drug delivery, gas storage, separation, and energy harvesting.
## 3 Crystal size and morphology
3 Crystal size and morphologyCurrent understanding on MOFs as crystalline materials revealed that they have well-defined molecular clusters (rather than from single atoms) of simple geometrical shapes known as secondary building units (SBUs) [2, 114–117]. Basically, SBU is a useful tool which was identified for the analysis of complex MOF structures. So far, MOF crystal structures have been described in the existing literature in various ways based on abstraction or deconstruction procedures [2, 114–117]. Application of such procedures is in fact no longer vague for simple symmetric metal-containing SBUs joined by ditopic or tritopic organic linkers. As such, the clusters can be easily abstracted in diverse shapes (such as triangles, squares, tetrahedra, and octahedra) to be linked into periodic structures. For example,
MOF-5 has octahedrally shaped \( \mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6} \) SBUs with six points of extension (carboxylate C atoms) connected by terephthalate (dicarboxylate) linkers, where the underlying net is a simple cubic structure that consists of six-coordinated (6-c) vertices with the symbol pcu, as shown in Fig. 9 [117].
The selection of key variables involved in the synthesis of MOFs (e.g., composition: selection of metal ions and organic linkers, process parameters, temperatures, additives, solvents, molar ratio of reactants, and reverse micro- or macroemulsions) can vary considerably to manipulate the size and morphology of MOF crystals in the micro/nano size ranges. The advantageous properties of MOF nanocrystals, e.g., the rate of adsorption, have been studied intensively to assess the influence of the particle size [114, 115]. As such, MOF nanocrystals have been a favorable choice for the fabrication of chemically tunable thin films with well-defined porosity, or smart membranes for catalytic coatings, chemical sensors, imaging, and drug delivery [114–119]. In the coming section, several crystal structures are discussed along with their essential synthesis processes. To date, various strategies have been adopted to optimize the physical properties of MOFs for specific applications, i.e., sensors, gas storage/separation, catalysis, and biomedical systems (Fig. 10) [120].
## 3.1 Compositional and process parameters
The composition and process parameters exert a tremendous influence on the size and morphology of
MOF crystals. For example, zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-78) is a prototypical mixed-ligand MOF, consisting of \( Z\mathbf{n}^{2+} \) ions bridged by 2-nitroimidazolate (nIm) and 5-nitrobenzimidazolate (nIIm) anions [118]. The influences of the nutrient (different DMF ratios) and ligand concentration (nIm/nIIm ratio) on the \( c \) axial growth of ZIF-78 were examined [44] (Fig. 11). Hexagonal rod-shaped crystals can be synthesized using TEA as the deprotonation agent. The size and aspect ratios of the ZIF-78 microrods were manipulated from "slender" to "squat" [44]. In a similar series, a straightforward method was reported for the efficient control of multiscale synthesis of \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) (BTC = benzene-1, 3,5-tricarboxylate) in the nano/micro regimes [119]. The nucleation rate of the \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) framework and the resulting crystal size were subtly controlled by adjusting the concentration of the dodecanoic acid additive. The small nanoparticles (50 nm) and the large crystals (1 \(\upmu\mathrm{m}\)) acquired from this comparative study showed almost the same BET surface area of \(1,270\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Since these values are the highest reported among \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) crystals [91], the results confirm
that concentrations of additive can exert direct effects on the size of MOFs without altering the BET surface area.
The control of size and morphology of \(\mathrm{Dy}(\mathrm{BTC})\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) crystals was also explored by combining coordination modulation with acid-base adjustment [121]. The size of these materials was modulated from micro- to nanometers solely by changing the amount of sodium acetate used in the synthesis process. With the increase of \(\mathrm{pH}\) (e.g., from 3.6 to 7.9) in the synthesis mixture, the dramatic change in morphology was also accompanied by a variation in the crystal size from \(40{-}60\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) to \(71\,\mathrm{nm}\), and eventually to several micrometers. These results indicated that the selection of a proper acid-base environment would crucially affect the final synthesis product [119]. Similarly, a direct solvothermal method was examined to produce \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53 (Al) crystals with controllable size and morphology simply by changing the amount of DMF and water [64]. A small amount of water promoted the deprotonation of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC in DMF, while excess water impeded its deprotonation. As the volume ratios of water in \(\mathrm{DMF-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) mixed solvents increased from \(0\;\mathrm{vol.}\%\) to \(25\;\mathrm{vol.}\%\), and then to \(100\;\mathrm{vol.}\%\), crystals with sizes ranging from \(1.65\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) to \(76\;\mathrm{nm}\) and then to \(520\;\mathrm{nm}\) with various morphologies were obtained (Fig. 12). The success of this approach suggests possible synthesis methods for MOFs yielding high crystallinity and few impurities. At the same time, disadvantages of these methods such as pore blocking caused by the capping agent should be refined.
## 3.2 Temperature
The nucleation and crystal growth in MOFs are extremely sensitive to temperature conditions at multiple stages during the synthesis process, including: (a) incubation of the reaction solution, (b) initiation of nucleation by heating at a high temperature, and (c) initiation of growth by cooling to a low temperature [120, 122, 123]. Jiang et al. [124] investigated the effect of temperature on the morphology and catalytic performance of Co-MOF-74 in \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-SCR formed via solvothermal synthesis over a low temperature range from 100 to \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 13). Interestingly, the Co-MOF-74 synthesized at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) attained a flower-like morphology with larger specific surface area and pore volume, while hexagonal prism structures were obtained at all other temperatures. The highest catalytic activity was obtained at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under the optimal synthesis temperature, which may be due to the combination of special morphology,
highest specific area as well as Co [124]. Zhu et al. [125] used temperatures of 180, 450, and \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to form \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) open-cell nanosheets. These have been later utilized as a metal oxide-organic framework foam in micro-reactor to directly degrade pollutants and simultaneously generate hydrogen.
## 3.3 Additives
The modulator effect on the synthesis of MOFs is found to be important in determining their properties (e.g., crystallinity, morphology, pore size, and gas separation performance). Essentially, a list of chemicals (e.g., acetic acid, benzoic acid, pyridine, and alkylamine) was reported to have been added to the reaction solution to adjust the size and shape of MOF crystals in many studies. These modulators were employed from the beginning stage of the reaction to influence the crystal growth through the adjustment of the reaction kinetics [119, 122, 126–130]. More recently, water-based modulated synthesis of \(\mathrm{Zr/Hf}\) MOFs was investigated in terms of
quantitative relationships between the optimal molar ratios (e.g., modulator vs. ligand) and their pKa values (Fig. 14) [130]. In addition, Drache et al. [131] investigated the modulator-driven polymorphism for Zr (IV) MOF synthesis, namely (Dresden University of Technology) DUT-126 (hbr), DUT-67 (reo), DUT-68 (bon), and DUT-69 (bct). Trifluoroacetic acid (Htfa) utilized as modulator in the formation of Zr(IV) MOFs affected the original topology, creating a product stable against hydrolysis. The nature and concentration of modulators are crucial in determining the size and morphology of MOFs. In addition, the use of additives or capping agents added to the reaction solution from the initial point of synthesis affects the ultimate crystal growth of MOF [119, 126, 131, 132]. For example, p-perfluoromethylbenzoic acid was utilized as an additive for MOF-5 synthesis to significantly affect its nucleation process. In addition, it also minimized the size of the nanocrystals of MOF-5 to \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) [122]. It should be noted that without the use of this additive, macroscopic crystallites larger than \(350\,\mathrm{nm}\) formed.
The use of some surfactant additives was also found to be effective to control the size and morphology of
MOF crystals [70, 106, 124, 133, 134]. Among those, CTAB was useful as a capping agent in the early incubation period to control the final crystal size and morphology. The crystal morphology and size of ZIF-8 were explored with the addition of diverse surfactants with similar
properties such as tetrapropylammonium bromide (TPABr), cetyltrimethylammonium chloride (CTAC) and trimethylstearylammonium chloride (STAC) during the synthesis procedure [70]. Among the surfactants, CTAB was found to be effective because of its longer hydrophobic hydrocarbon chain structure. This hydrophobic part was used as capping agent on the surface of ZIF-8. Thus, the crystal growth of ZIF-8 has been restricted.
The use of sodium acetate and glycerol as modulators was examined as a means to precisely control the size and morphology of iron-terephthalic acid MOFs [41]. The authors obtained octahedron, bipyramidal hexagonal, and spindle shapes by adjusting the quantities of solvents and deprotonating agents. Although the above three shapes of MOFs showed similar chemical components (e.g., iron, carbon, and oxygen atoms), their specific surface areas differed greatly(e.g., 1157, 787.8, and \(13.75\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively). The nucleation rate and orientational growth of these MOFs were found to play dominant roles in determining their shapes [41].
## 3.4 Reverse micro- and macroemulsions
A reverse macroemulsion method involves dispersion of water in oil (abbreviated as w/o) to enrich the synthesis strategies of nanosized MOFs. The synthesis strategies of other nanoscale zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) or MOFs are promising for applications in biology related fields and even in traditional selective separation and catalysis. For example, Tao et al. [135] reported facile and rapid synthesis of an \(\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{III})\)-containing nanoscale MOF under ultrasonication using reverse macroemulsion. The selection of the specific ratio between the components (e.g., metal ions, organic linkers, and solvent) and their ordered addition is important to determine not only the morphology and size but also the photoluminescent properties of MOFs, which can critically expand their applicability [136]. More recently, Sun et al. [137] reported the use of reverse microemulsions for the synthesis of nanoscale ZIFs such as ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 with uniform grain size distribution and extremely small crystal dimensions (mean particle size less than \(5\,\mathrm{nm}\)) at room temperature. Compared to the samples obtained from water, ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 synthesized by reverse microemulsion exhibited much larger surface areas and micropore volumes.
## 4 Challenges in controlling MOF synthesis
## 4.1 Solvents as structure-directing agents
For the rational synthesis and design of MOF materials, many interesting and effective approaches have been introduced and attempted in the contexts described above. For the successful functioning of porous materials, it is critical to offer structural variations with different apertures and shapes. Significant efforts have been devoted to the controllable synthesis of desired polymers [138]. Structure-directing agents play a dominant role, particularly during the self-assembly process where slight differences of metal ions or bridging ligands can lead to the formation of different structures. As structure-directing agents, three different solvents including DMF, DEF, and isopropanol (PrOH) were employed to synthesize three types of novel three-dimensional (3D) coordination polymers. These structures were formed by the self-assembly of a flexible tricarboxylate ligand and a \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\) metal ion, specifically (1) \([\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{tc})(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})]\cdot\mathrm{MF},\) (2) \([\mathrm{Cd}_{3}(\mathrm{tc})_{2}(\mathrm{DEF})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{DEF}\) and, (3) \([\mathrm{Cd}_{1.5}(\mathrm{tci})(\mathrm{PrOH})]\cdot\mathrm{PrOH}\) (where \(\mathrm{tci}=\mathrm{tris}(2\cdot\mathrm{carboxyethyl})\) isocyanurate), [139]. Interestingly, three different kinds of topology structures will only form by changing the solvent types. Additionally, compound (1) exhibited an anionic framework, while compounds (2) and (3) possessed neutral frameworks. This report clearly indicated that solvents may play a crucial role in the synthesis of various types of frameworks. For example, a facile and controllable synthesis approach was reported for the porous \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]\) framework (where \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}=1,1^{\prime}:3,1^{\prime}\)-terphenyl-4,4',5'-tricarboxylic acid) (NMOF 1) [100]. It is worth noting that the volume ratio of the solvents: DMF and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) exerted remarkable effect on the size of NMOF 1. Information regarding the volume of DMF and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) used for the synthesis and the resulting BET surface area of crystals is summarized in Table 4. With increasing the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) volume fraction from 0.1/20 to 4/20, the morphology of NMOF 1 changed dramatically. Meanwhile, the size of these crystals, assessed in terms of length, increased from 2 to \(18\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\). More importantly, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption properties of NMOF microcrystals varied in relation to the crystal morphologies and exposed surfaces. For instance, the tetragonal plate particles demonstrated lower \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption capacity on the predominantly exposed \(\{004\}\) facets. In contrast,
the elongated tetragonal bipyramidal crystals demonstrated greater \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption capability, as their exposed {101} facets were accessible for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption. This study prompted the control of the size and morphology of MOF materials fabricated for capturing specific gases.
The uptake pattern of synthesized nano and micro ZIF-8 crystals (70 nm and 20 μm, respectively) was assessed against two types of targets, such as \(\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\)ions and Rhodamine B [140]. The larger ZIF-8 particles were fabricated from \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) and 2-methylimidazole (H-MeIM) in DMF, while their nanosized counterparts were modulated by Span 80 and Tween 80. For the ZIF-8 nanocrystals (BET: \(1,360\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)), the adsorption of \(\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\)was nearly completed in approximately 10 min, while the micron particles (BET: \(1,560\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) reached adsorption equilibrium at 240 min. Interestingly the final concentrations of \(\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\)were almost same for both sizes of ZIF-8 particles. Rhodamine B, is a larger molecule than the aperture size of nanosized ZIF-8, and ZIF-8 micron crystals showed almost no adsorption. It is thus likely that smaller crystals with a relatively high external surface area-to-volume ratio will be more advantageous in applications like gas storage, catalysis, and drug delivery.
In another series, the tri-connected achiral ligand pyridine-3,5-dicarboxylate (3,5-PDC) was chosen to
synthesize three new types of MOFs, including Mn(3,5-PDC) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) (glycol) (JUC-58, where JUC = Jilin University, China) and M(3,5-PDC) \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) (glycol)· \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) (JUC-59-M, \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\) or Ni) using a solvothermal method with DMF and glycol [141]. Note that the DMF and glycol solvents are often suggested to play a crucial role in the formation of MOF network structures. JUC-58 represents an unusual example of a 3D chiral MOF structure with helical channels, while JUC-59-Co and JUC-59-Ni are isostructural non-centrosymmetric MOFs with a rare 3-connected non-interpenetrated lig-b net [141]. These unique findings deserve further focus to enrich the design strategy for novel porous materials.
## 4.2 Precursor approach
For the development of MOFs with unique properties (e.g., high stability and high porosity), the nature of the precursor was an important factor to consider. Consequently, it is essential to screen appropriate precursors for synthesizing MOFs. Recently, Laurikėnas et al. [142] reported iron (III) as a precursor obtained using an acetic/hydrogen peroxide-type synthesis, for the fabrication of mixed MOFs. First, crude iron (III) acetate crystals were obtained from iron (II) powder and acetic acid using hydrogen peroxide. Second, the
crude product was purified by recrystallization in EtOH. Finally, the purified iron (III) acetate was used for the fabrication of \(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O(F_{4}BDC)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}\cdot(D M F)_{3.5}}\) with 2,3,4,5- etrafluor-benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid in a mixed EtOH/DMF solution. Ultimately, the recrystallized iron (III) acetate crystals were successfully employed as the precursor for the synthesis of mixed MOFs \([\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{F}_{4}\mathrm{BDC})_{3}\) \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}\cdot\mathrm{DMF}]_{3.5}]\) with improved thermal stability [142– 144].
## 4.3 In-situ linker synthesis
Among the various MOF synthesis methods, in-situ ligand synthesis is of growing interest recently due to its simplicity, cost effectiveness, and environmental friendliness. A new 2-methylimidazolate-4-amide-5- imidate linker was synthesized using an in-situ method involving the partial hydrolysis of 4,5-dicyano-2- methylimidazole under solvothermal conditions. The first zinc-organic framework was then synthesized based on an imidazolate-amid-imidate [145]. Subsequently, the neutral microporous imidazolate MOF \([Z\mathrm{n}(2)]_{n}\) with one-dimensional (1D) hexagonal channels was formed by linking the upper new ligand with \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{++}\) ions. Due to suitable channels with polar walls, the imidazolate-MOF (IMOF) obtained, with an estimated channel diameter of \(3.82\,\mathring\mathrm{A},\) could potentially be used as a molecular sieve for the separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO},\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4},\) and \(C O_{2}/C O\) (the kinetic diameters of CO and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) are 3.76 and \(3.80\mathrm{~\AA~}\) respectively). These results reflected the importance of screening MOF materials with appropriate sizes for separating mixtures of gases in industrial settings and not only. It should also be noted that both the physisorption caused by the pore size in concert with the chemisorption caused by acid-base neutralization reaction need to be considered for some MOFs with other properties.
For the synthesis of a porous coordination network: PCN-46, polyyne-coupled di-isophthalate ligand was formed in-situ using the copper (I)-catalyzed oxidative coupling of terminal acetylenes [146]. As the isophthalate ligand bearing terminal acetylene is bound with a slight excess of copper (II) salt, it can be readily reduced to copper (I) under solvothermal reaction conditions. The homocoupled product, di-isophthalate, can then be fabricated. Comparing with two other NbO type MOFs with similar ligands, namely NOTT-101 and NOTT-
102, it was apparent that the BET surface areas would increase from 2,316 to \(2,942\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with the increase of the ligand length from 5.773 to \(10.098\mathrm{~\AA},\) respectively.
## 4.4 Control of catenation
In many MOF syntheses, an increase of the bridging ligand length often results in larger channels and pores in MOF materials. As a high degree of catenation tends to diminish the porosity of MOFs, they are proven to be advantageous for chemical catalysis [147] and high-pressure gas storage [148]. An effective method to control catenation in MOFs was also proposed via rational design of the organic building block (robust tetratopic carboxylates) [149]. It was suggested that the deprotonated \(^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}\) -benzene-1,2,4,5-tetrayl-tetrabenzoic acid (L5) would favor the formation of relatively large cavities, which can suppress the formation of catenated MOFs. It was then proven to produce a noncatenated MOF from a solvothermal method using L5 and \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in DMF at \(80\,^{\circ}C\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Moreover, L5 was found to form pillared paddlewheel MOFs. The MOFs prepared in this fashion can be used to fabricate MOF materials with noncatenated [150] and catenated structures [151].
## 4.5 Functionalization methods
It is well understood that the porosity and surface properties of MOFs can be altered by modulating the size and shape of the organic linkers or attaching various functional groups. In this respect, a modulation method was proposed to obtain two types of compounds: (1) \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) one-dimensional nanorods and (2) \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanotubes. These compounds were synthesized from copper (II) and terephthalic/ammonotephthalic acid with triethylenediamine as the pillar ligand and acetic acid as the modulator [152]. However, when the ligand BDC was replaced by \(\mathrm{BDC}\mathrm{-NH}_{2},\) a new nanotube morphology of MOF materials was formed instead of nanorods (Fig. 15). More importantly, with the switch from BDC to \(\mathrm{BDC}\mathrm{-NH}_{2},\) the adsorption isotherm of the compound also changed from Type I to Type IV [152]. This may be due to the presence of nanotubes, which provide some mesoporosity in compound (2).
A facile synthesis approach was reported for seven new functionalized aluminum terephthalates: \([\mathrm{Al}_{3}\mathrm{OCl}(\mathrm{DEF})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC}-\) \(\mathrm{X})_{3}]\) where \(\mathrm{X=-CH_{3},1-CH_{3};-NO_{2},2-NO_{2};-OCH_{3},3-OCH_{3};}\)
\(-\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4},\,4\mathrm{-C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4};\,-\mathrm{F}_{2},\,5\mathrm{-F}_{2};\,-(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2},\,6\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2};\,-(\mathrm{OCH}_{3})_{2},\) \(7\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OCH}_{3})_{2}\) . The gas adsorption behaviors of the above compounds were compared in a number of respects [153]. Each synthesized compounds showed different thermal stability: specifically \(4\mathrm{-C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) \((430\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\,>\,5\mathrm{-F}_{2}\) \((410\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})>1\mathrm{-CH}_{3}\) \((400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})>6\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\) \((370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})>3\mathrm{-OCH}_{3}\) \((350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})>2\mathrm{-NO}_{2}\) \((300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})>7\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OCH}_{3})_{2}\) \((260\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . In this experiment, mono-functionalized Al-MIL-101-X compounds had higher BET surface areas than those with
di-functionalized variants, namely \(1\mathrm{-CH}_{3}>\) Al-MIL\(101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}>3\mathrm{-OCH}_{3}>7\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OCH}_{3})_{2}>2\mathrm{-NO}_{2}\approx6\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}>4\mathrm{-C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}>5\mathrm{-F}_{2}\) . It was speculated that the adsorption capacities were dependent on the size and nature of various functional groups. These findings remind us that there may be more efficient methods of synthesizing functional MOF materials with appropriate stability and BET surface area.
## 5 Future application prospects
As a new class of porous materials, MOFs have attracted a great deal of attention due to their potential applications in diverse fields. Hence, a constant creation of new functionalized materials offered opportunities to broaden their application fields. On the other hand, to further expand such possibilities, the improvement in their modification techniques (e.g., effective control of their sizes and morphologies in relation to their applicability and potential performance) can also help enrich the development of MOFs and other related materials. More importantly, the specific applications of the same MOFs in many different ways have been verified with the modification of their size and morphology: a) gas adsorption differences of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) MOFs [46], such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\); b) drug adsorption behaviors of \(\gamma\)-cyclodextrin MOFs [154–156]; c) separation performance [157]; and d) morphology-dependent catalytic performance [158, 159]. As MOF materials with different properties are newly developed, many smart MOFs are also expected in near future.
of MOFs. Lastly, we believe that the development of novel techniques required for the effective control of the size and morphology of MOFs will be impactful enough to make a significant contribution to the broadening of their applications.
